// InlineLeaves — a Std→Std pass that replaces a direct call to a TINY LEAF function with a COPY of
// that function's body, and leaves the callee where it is.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY A CALL TO A THREE-OP FUNCTION IS THE THING TO DELETE.** MEASURED 2026-08-26 on the stage-1
// self-compile (`temp/r6/`): `regMaskContains` — `((mask shr regNum) and 1) != 0`, plus the entry guard
// its `int(0 to 63)` parameter earns — is **21.07% of the profile's self time** from **14 static call
// sites**. The call is not most of its cost; it is nearly all of it, because the body is smaller than
// the protocol around it (the argument moves, the `call`, the frame the guard's panic block forces the
// prologue pass to give it, the `ret`). The same census finds **406 strict leaves** and 3,704 sites into
// leaves of ≤ 12 body ops — 2.6% of the program's calls STATICALLY, and a far larger share dynamically,
// because a leaf that small is what a loop calls.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE DEPARTURE FROM v1 THAT MAKES THIS RUNG POSSIBLE AT ALL IS THE PANIC RULE.**
// `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Passes/Inliner.maxon` is a general Std-tier inliner — 3,067 lines, a
// 5-round fixpoint, loop-depth and const-argument multipliers, SCC recursion refusal, cross-module
// stdlib snapshots and ~1,500 lines of refcount side-table surgery — and it REFUSES any callee that can
// panic (`calleeProducesObservableFrame`, :915-943), to keep `range-check-panic` / `panic.in-function`'s
// stack traces exact. Under that rule EVERY ranged-parameter leaf is excluded, `regMaskContains`
// included: its `RegNum` guard ends in a panic block. So the rule that protected v1's traces is the rule
// that would have excluded this rung's whole reason for existing. See `mintSlowArm` for what replaces
// it, and why the traces are still exact.
//
// The rest of v1's machinery is deliberately absent, each for its own reason:
//   • **the fixpoint and the multipliers** (:43-115) — one round, leaf-only. A cascade is what pulled
//     `Math.pow`'s 70 ops through three helpers in v1; the profile says the leaves that matter are tiny
//     in every loop, so a size heuristic buys nothing here that a flat budget does not.
//   • **every ownership side table** (:1217-1614, :2619-2846) — shv2 inserts refcounts at the MAXON
//     tier, so a retain, a release and a scope drop are all `call` ops and a body holding one is not a
//     leaf. There is nothing to thread.
//   • **cross-module snapshots** (`PassPipeline.maxon:518-562`) — shv2 compiles `stdlib/` into the same
//     module. There is nothing to snapshot.
//   • **the slot-op refusal** (:1660) — `stackRecordAddr` is `isUnsupportedInInlineBody`, and
//     `promoteStackRecords` runs AFTER this pass, so none exists yet either way.
//   • **`BlockArgReturnRole` tagging** — shv2 declares the field and nothing reads it. Wiring it here
//     would be a producer with no consumer.
//
// What IS taken from v1: the dense remap COLUMN (:2356-2455) and the two binding phases it runs in, and
// the reason its parameter phase scans the WHOLE entry block rather than a leading run of `param` ops
// (:2346-2355) — a scan that stops at the first non-param op binds nothing when anything is prepended,
// and every spliced body op then reads the CALLER's same-numbered value. shv2 needs no scan at all for
// that: `ValueId`s `0..paramCount-1` ARE the parameters by the Std ABI's own convention
// (`IrValueId.maxon`, and `InsertRangeChecks.rangedParamValue` rests on the same identity), so the
// column's parameter half is a range rather than a search — and the `param` ops the copy skips are
// CHECKED against that convention as they go by (`assertParamOpMatchesConvention`).

// ⭐ **THE OP BUDGET, AND WHAT IT ADMITS — MEASURED, at this tier, not guessed from the target dump.**
// Counted over all of a callee's blocks with terminators included and `param` ops excluded (a `param`
// becomes the call's argument rather than an instruction).
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE REASON 24 WAS CHOSEN IS GONE. THE NUMBER IS NOT — IT NOW RESTS ON A DIFFERENT MEASUREMENT,
// AND THE TWO MUST NOT BE CONFUSED.** It was 24 because `regMaskContains` was 23 Std ops and 16 refused
// it: the headline function of the rung that set this budget, refused by its own budget. What put it at
// 23 was where THE SHIFT RULE lives — `Parser.emitGuardedShift` saturates a shift whose count the
// compiler cannot fold, at the MAXON tier, in ops. **EC6 deleted that for this leaf**: a count whose
// DECLARED alias lies inside the counts the instruction takes as written is now emitted bare
// (`Parser.shiftCountIsProvenUnguarded`), `regNum` is `int(0 to 63)`, and `((mask shr regNum) and 1) != 0`
// **fits inside 16** — MEASURED, not inferred: the leaf inlines at a budget of 16.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT HOLDS 24 UP TODAY IS THE SITE COUNT ALONE.** MEASURED on the self-compile (2026-08-27, both
// arms after EC6): at **24**, 3,673 sites inlined across 418 eligible callees, 512 refused by size across
// 49 callees; at **16**, 3,286 sites across 349 callees, 899 refused across 118, of which 387 sites / 69
// callees a budget of 24 would admit. **24 buys 387 sites and 69 callees over 16** — a tenth more
// inlining. That is not a rounding error, so LOWERING this back is a change in its own right and owes its
// own CPU A/B; it may not ride on the justification above, which has been spent.
//
// ⚠ Both arms were read on the binary EC6's review then CORRECTED (`Parser.shiftCountIsProvenUnguarded`
// was spending a merge's declared alias as a proof), so the 24 arm was RE-READ afterwards and is
// unchanged to the digit — 3,673 / 418 / 512 / 49 on the fixed tree. The 16 arm was not re-run: it needs
// its own build, and the correction only ever RE-ADDS a guard, which can move that arm's numbers down
// but not the conclusion, since 24 admits every leaf 16 does.
//
// The pass reports what `ReportedLargerLeafBudget` would additionally admit, so the next person to ask
// has the number for THIS compile instead of a guess.
let MaxInlinedLeafOps = 24 as OpCount

// The second budget the pass MEASURES but does not use — reported beside the refusals so that "would a
// bigger one be better?" is answered from the compile being asked about rather than re-derived. It is
// deliberately not a knob: two budgets that both took effect would be two rules.
let ReportedLargerLeafBudget = 32 as OpCount

// The block labels this pass mints. They reach the committed goldens, so they are short and prefixed
// `__il_` (inline-leaves — disjoint from `InsertRangeChecks`' `__rc_` and `InlineManagedPrimitives`'
// `__im_`).
//
// ⭐ **A COPIED block KEEPS THE CALLEE BLOCK'S OWN LABEL — except the ENTRY, and that exception is the
// rule.** `__rc_ok`, `ifcont`, `whilehdr` are claims about a CONSTRUCT, and every one of them is still
// true after the block is copied into another function; `entry` is a claim about a FUNCTION, and a copied
// block is not the caller's entry. Left alone it put a second block called `entry` inside `main`
// (MEASURED — `a-pure-leaf-is-inlined`'s golden read `entry` / `entry#0`, the printer disambiguating two
// blocks of one name), which is exactly the label a reader trusts not to be ambiguous.
let InlineContLabel = b"__il_cont"
let InlineSlowLabel = b"__il_slow"
let InlineBodyLabel = b"__il_body"

typealias SiteCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// How many parameter cascades one callee carries — its arity at most, and one in nearly every case.
typealias GuardCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// The shared empties a `LeafInliner` starts with and NEVER writes to — `seedSiteBuffers` replaces them
// with real arrays at the first site, and `valueIds` replaces the minter at the first splice. This pass
// constructs one inliner per FUNCTION in the module and the overwhelming majority of functions hold no
// inlinable site at all, so three `create()`s in the constructor would be three heap objects per
// function for nothing. Same shape, and the same measured reason, as
// `InlineManagedPrimitives.sharedUnseededMinter` and `InsertRangeChecks`' chain columns; safe for the
// same reason — the one operation that would write to each is the one that replaces it.
let sharedEmptySitePositions = BlockOpPosArray.create()
let sharedEmptySiteCallees = FuncIndexArray.create()
let sharedUnseededLeafMinter = ValueMinter.create(0)

// ⭐ The two columns a splice that declines NOTHING never writes to — which is nearly every splice in the
// module, since a callee with a narrowed parameter is a handful of them. Shared for the three empties
// above's reason and with their invariant: `DeclinedGuards.decline` REPLACES both fields before its first
// write, so neither is ever mutated. MEASURED on the scale ladder before they existed: **+34,575
// `phase:inlineLeaves` allocations at rung 5, doubling per rung** — two arrays per splice, for a plan
// almost every splice answers `false` from.
let sharedEmptyDeclineColumn = BoolArray.create()
let sharedEmptyForwardColumn = BlockIdArray.create()

// --- what ONE op of a candidate body means for the leaf rule ---
enum LeafOpRole
	// Copyable verbatim into the caller.
	body
	// A `param`: not counted against the budget and never copied — the splice maps its value to the
	// call site's argument instead.
	parameter
	// A call of ANY kind. It refuses the callee outright, and it is the rule that makes ownership free
	// here (a retain/release/scope-drop is a `call`) and recursion impossible (a self-call is a call).
	calling
	// `osPanic` / `osPanicValue`. Its BLOCK is dropped and redirected to the slow arm, but only if the
	// whole body is pure — see `mintSlowArm`.
	panicking
	// An op the dialect itself marks unsupported inside an inline body — or one that can FAULT, which
	// the dialect marks only in prose. See `leafOpRole`.
	refusing
end 'LeafOpRole'

// ⭐ **THE ROLE OF ONE OP — one exhaustive `match`, and the TAIL of it reads the dialect's own flag.**
// The variants named explicitly are the ones whose role is NOT derivable from
// `isUnsupportedInInlineBody`: the five calls and `param` all carry `false` for it (a call is perfectly
// representable inside a body — it just is not a LEAF's body), the two panics carry `true` but are
// handled rather than refused, and `div`/`mod` carry `false` while being the only ops that can FAULT.
// Everything else answers off the flag, which is what gives that field its first reader and means an op
// added with it set is refused here without this file learning its name.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A DIVIDE REFUSES THE CALLEE, AND THE REASON IS THE PANIC RULE'S OWN — ONE CASE FURTHER OUT.**
// `div`/`mod` lower to `idiv`, which raises `#DE` on divide-by-zero and on `i64.min / -1`; the fault
// handler prints `panic: integer overflow` with a symbolized backtrace, and that backtrace is taken from
// WHERE THE INSTRUCTION IS. A panic OP has a slow arm this pass can re-issue the call through, so its
// frame survives; a faulting instruction has nothing to re-issue — moving it moves the frame, full stop.
// MEASURED: `safety/integer-overflow-fault-from-int-min-over-minus-one` read `in main / in mrt_start`
// where it pins `in divide / in main / in mrt_start`, because `divide` is a two-op leaf.
// (`loadIndirect` can fault too — a null receiver — but the language promises no message and no trace
// for that, and refusing every leaf that reads memory would refuse `Array.isEmpty` and `ValueMinter.mint`
// along with it. The line is drawn where a DIAGNOSTIC is promised, which is exactly where a spec pins one.)
//
// ⚠ Every other variant is covered by a RANGE arm ending at the union's last member, so a variant
// APPENDED past it fails to compile here until it is consciously placed — the band-append invariant
// working in the reviewer's favour.
function leafOpRole(op StdOp) returns LeafOpRole
	match op 'kind'
		const to cmpImm then break 'kind'
		div to mod then return LeafOpRole.refusing
		condBranch to ret then break 'kind'
		param then return LeafOpRole.parameter
		call then return LeafOpRole.calling
		retVoid to rdataAddr then break 'kind'
		tryCall then return LeafOpRole.calling
		errorReturn to funcAddr then break 'kind'
		callIndirect then return LeafOpRole.calling
		osPanic then return LeafOpRole.panicking
		osReadClock to switch then break 'kind'
		witnessCall then return LeafOpRole.calling
		osPipeCreate to osReadWallClock then break 'kind'
		witnessTryCall then return LeafOpRole.calling
		osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then break 'kind'
		osPanicValue then return LeafOpRole.panicking
		// The allocator's page/fill/atomic band and the scheduler's thread/TLS band (F1, F2, G1) are placed
		// here consciously: none is a call site, a terminator or a `param`, so each one's own
		// `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` answers below — a fill and the two atomics are body ops a leaf may
		// hold, the import calls refuse.
		osCommandLine to osEnterBackgroundPriority then break 'kind'
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then break 'kind'
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then break 'kind'
		loadAcquire or storeRelease then break 'kind'
		osHandleFileType then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'

	return LeafOpRole.refusing if op.rawValue.isUnsupportedInInlineBody else LeafOpRole.body
end 'leafOpRole'

// --- the per-callee verdict ---
enum LeafVerdict
	// The body may be spliced at any direct call site that is not its own function.
	eligible
	// ⭐ THE PANIC RULE'S REFUSAL: the body panics AND writes memory. Re-running it on the slow arm
	// would repeat the write, so it is refused rather than inlined-with-a-redirect.
	panicWithStore
	// Inside every other rule but past `MaxInlinedLeafOps`. Kept apart from `notALeaf` because it is the
	// only refusal a BUDGET could change, and the report says how many sites it costs.
	tooManyOps
	// Everything else, and the list is the whole eligibility rule read backwards: a call of any kind, a
	// `/` or `mod` (which can FAULT — see `leafOpRole`), an `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` op, a
	// by-reference parameter, a body that carries the green-thread stack guard, a panicking ENTRY block,
	// an entry block carrying phis, a block still in `Terminator.unset` (a body the lowering never
	// emitted), or no body at all.
	notALeaf
end 'LeafVerdict'

typealias LeafVerdictArray = Array with LeafVerdict

// What one scan of a candidate body found. A record rather than two out-params because the verdict is a
// JOIN of several facts and the budget question needs the op count even when the answer is `eligible`.
type LeafBodyScan
	export var verdict as LeafVerdict
	export var opCount as OpCount

	export static function create(verdict LeafVerdict, opCount OpCount) returns LeafBodyScan
		return Self{verdict: verdict, opCount: opCount}
	end 'create'
end 'LeafBodyScan'

// ⭐ **EVERY FUNCTION'S VERDICT, COMPUTED ONCE, ON THE PRE-SPLICE BODY.** v1 recomputed spliceability per
// SITE (`Inliner.maxon:145-171` records that as `callSites × calleeSize`, one of its two measured
// quadratics); here the scan is one walk of the module and every site is a column read.
//
// ⭐⭐ **PRE-SPLICE IS ALSO THE SEMANTICS, NOT MERELY THE SCHEDULE.** Because the verdicts are all taken
// before the first splice, a caller that BECOMES call-free by being inlined into does not become a leaf
// in this compile. That is the "one round, no cascade" rule, and it is what bounds the work at one copy
// per site — stated here rather than left to be inferred from the order of two loops.
type LeafPlan
	var verdicts as LeafVerdictArray
	// One past the highest ValueId each ELIGIBLE callee's body uses — the width of the value column a
	// splice builds. Zero for every other function; `nextFreeValueId` materializes a live-op index array,
	// so it is asked only where an answer will be spent.
	var valueBounds as ValueIdArray
	// The same membership as `verdicts == eligible`, in the shape `ValueOriginTable.indexRowsOfFunctions`
	// takes. Built in the same walk rather than derived later, so the two cannot disagree about which
	// callees' origin rows are indexed.
	export var eligibleFuncs as BoolArray
	// `verdicts == tooManyOps` AND inside `ReportedLargerLeafBudget` — a BIT rather than the op count it
	// is derived from, because the count has exactly one reader and this is what that reader asks.
	var withinLargerBudget as BoolArray
	export var eligibleCount as FuncIndex
	export var panicWithStoreCount as FuncIndex
	export var tooManyOpsCount as FuncIndex
	export var tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget as FuncIndex

	static function create(funcCount FuncIndex) returns LeafPlan
		var valueBounds = ValueIdArray.create()
		valueBounds.resize(funcCount)
		var eligibleFuncs = BoolArray.create()
		eligibleFuncs.resize(funcCount)
		var withinLargerBudget = BoolArray.create()
		withinLargerBudget.resize(funcCount)
		return Self{
			verdicts: LeafVerdictArray.create(),
			valueBounds: valueBounds,
			eligibleFuncs: eligibleFuncs,
			withinLargerBudget: withinLargerBudget,
			eligibleCount: 0,
			panicWithStoreCount: 0,
			tooManyOpsCount: 0,
			tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget: 0
		}
	end 'create'

	// File function `funcIndex`'s verdict. Called once per function, in index order, so `verdicts` is
	// PUSHED — an `Array with <enum>` is a struct array, and `resize` sets such an array's length without
	// giving its slots storage (`CompileTimings.create` records that trap for `Array with PhaseCost`).
	function record(funcIndex FuncIndex, scan LeafBodyScan, module StdModule, func IrFunction)
		if funcIndex != self.verdicts.count() 'outOfOrder'
			panic("LeafPlan.record: asked for function {funcIndex} with {self.verdicts.count()} already recorded — the verdict column is pushed in index order, so it IS the function numbering")
		end 'outOfOrder'

		self.verdicts.push(scan.verdict)
		match scan.verdict 'tally'
			eligible then self.recordEligible(funcIndex, module: module, func: func)
			panicWithStore then self.panicWithStoreCount = self.panicWithStoreCount + 1
			tooManyOps then self.recordTooManyOps(funcIndex, opCount: scan.opCount)
			notALeaf then break 'tally'
		end 'tally'
	end 'record'

	function recordEligible(funcIndex FuncIndex, module StdModule, func IrFunction)
		self.eligibleCount = self.eligibleCount + 1
		try self.eligibleFuncs.set(funcIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.recordEligible: eligibleFuncs.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
		try self.valueBounds.set(funcIndex, value: nextFreeValueId(module, func: func)) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.recordEligible: valueBounds.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
	end 'recordEligible'

	function recordTooManyOps(funcIndex FuncIndex, opCount OpCount)
		self.tooManyOpsCount = self.tooManyOpsCount + 1
		if opCount > ReportedLargerLeafBudget 'pastEvenTheLargerBudget'
			return
		end 'pastEvenTheLargerBudget'

		self.tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget = self.tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget + 1
		try self.withinLargerBudget.set(funcIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.recordTooManyOps: withinLargerBudget.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
	end 'recordTooManyOps'

	export function verdictOf(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns LeafVerdict
		return try self.verdicts.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.verdictOf: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.verdicts.count()}-function module this plan was built over")
	end 'verdictOf'

	export function isWithinLargerBudget(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
		return try self.withinLargerBudget.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.isWithinLargerBudget: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.withinLargerBudget.count()}-function module this plan was built over")
	end 'isWithinLargerBudget'

	export function valueBoundOf(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns ValueId
		return try self.valueBounds.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.valueBoundOf: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.valueBounds.count()}-function module this plan was built over")
	end 'valueBoundOf'
end 'LeafPlan'

function buildLeafPlan(module StdModule) returns LeafPlan
	var plan = LeafPlan.create(module.funcCount() as FuncIndex)
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
		plan.record(funcIndex, scan: scanLeafBody(module, func: func), module: module, func: func)
	end 'eachFunc'
	return plan
end 'buildLeafPlan'

// One candidate body's whole verdict, from one walk.
//
// ⚠ **IT WALKS `blockRefs` → `opRefs` + terminator DIRECTLY rather than `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, and
// that is the same descent** — but the array version MATERIALIZES the index list, and this runs over
// EVERY function in the module including the large majority that answer `notALeaf` on their first call
// op. `InlineManagedPrimitives.expandChainFrom` walks the same halves for the same reason. A terminator
// is never a `param` and never a panic, but it IS counted and it CAN be an `errorReturn`, so the half
// this walk would otherwise omit is exactly where a throwing body is caught.
function scanLeafBody(module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns LeafBodyScan
	if func.blockRefs.count() == 0 'noBody'
		return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: 0)
	end 'noBody'
	// A by-reference parameter is an ADDRESS whose aliasing is not what a tiny leaf is, and a body that
	// carries the green-thread stack guard has a load-bearing FRAME — inlining it into an exempt caller
	// erases the checkpoint (`IrFunction.needsGreenThreadStackGuard`). Both are function-level facts, so
	// they are asked before a single op is read.
	if func.reassignedParamMask != 0 or func.needsGreenThreadStackGuard 'refusedBySignature'
		return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: 0)
	end 'refusedBySignature'

	var ops = 0 as OpCount
	var hasPanic = false
	var hasStore = false
	for blockIndex in 0 upto func.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
		let blockRef = try func.blockRefs.get(blockIndex) otherwise panic("scanLeafBody: blockRefs.get OOB at {blockIndex} inside its own count")
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("scanLeafBody: blockRef {blockRef} of '{String.from(func.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
		// The ENTRY block's phis would have to be fed by the caller's branch into the copy, and there is
		// nothing to feed them with — a function's entry takes its inputs as `param` ops. The parser mints
		// no entry phi, so this is a stated invariant rather than a case; a body that broke it would
		// otherwise be spliced with its entry args unbound.
		if blockIndex == 0 and block.blockArgs.count() != 0 'entryCarriesPhis'
			return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: 0)
		end 'entryCarriesPhis'
		// ⛔⛔ **A BLOCK STILL IN `Terminator.unset` MEANS THE BODY WAS NEVER LOWERED, AND THERE IS
		// NOTHING TO COPY.** `LowerMaxonToStd` RELOCATES every function's blocks and then SKIPS the
		// bodies of the stdlib functions no path from `main` reaches — the function is still pushed
		// (`FunctionNameIndex` counts it for E4015) but not one of its ops, not even its `param` ops, is
		// emitted. What is left looks exactly like a zero-op leaf: no call, no store, no panic, inside
		// any budget. MEASURED before this rule existed: `String.byteLength` reached `carryOplessExit`
		// with an `unset` entry block, and `Array.clone` reached `buildValueColumn` with a 2-parameter
		// signature over a 1-wide value space — 64 spec workers died across the suite between them.
		//
		// The test is `isTerminated`, not `hasTerminatorOp`: a `dead` block is legitimately op-less and
		// IS copied (see `carryOplessExit`), and `unset` is the one state that says "unwritten".
		if not block.isTerminated() 'bodyWasNeverLowered'
			return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: 0)
		end 'bodyWasNeverLowered'

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("scanLeafBody: op index {opIdx} named by a block of '{String.from(func.name)}' is not in module.ops")
			// Bound ONCE: this runs on every op of every function in the module, and the role is a `match`
			// over a union with ninety variants.
			let role = leafOpRole(op)
			match role 'role'
				// A parameter is the call's argument, not an instruction — it is neither counted nor copied.
				parameter then break 'role'
				// ⭐ THE STORE TEST READS THE DIALECT'S OWN FLAG, so a new memory op inherits the panic rule
				// without this file learning its name. It is asked only of `body` ops: a panic op's `isStore`
				// records that it ends the process, which is precisely the effect the slow arm exists to let
				// happen exactly once, and a refusing op's is moot.
				body then hasStore = hasStore or op.rawValue.isStore
				panicking then hasPanic = true
				// ⭐ **RETURNED FROM, NOT FLAGGED — a call is the commonest op there is, and this scan runs
				// over the whole module.** Bailing at the first one is what keeps a 5,000-op function from
				// being walked to the end to learn something its second op already said.
				calling then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
				refusing then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
			end 'role'
			// A panic in the ENTRY block leaves nothing to inline — the head would branch to a block the
			// splice does not copy — so it refuses rather than redirects.
			if role == LeafOpRole.panicking and blockIndex == 0 'entryPanics'
				return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
			end 'entryPanics'

			ops = ops + countedBodyOps(role)
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'terminated'
			let term = try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("scanLeafBody: terminator op index of a block of '{String.from(func.name)}' is not in module.ops")
			ops = ops + 1
			match leafOpRole(term) 'termRole'
				// A terminator is never a parameter and never a panic; both would be a malformed block, and
				// saying so here is cheaper than a rule elsewhere that has to remember it.
				parameter then panic("scanLeafBody: '{String.from(func.name)}' has a `param` op in a TERMINATOR slot")
				panicking then panic("scanLeafBody: '{String.from(func.name)}' has a panic op in a TERMINATOR slot — a panic is a body op followed by an unreachable return (`InsertRangeChecks.emitPanicBlock`)")
				body then break 'termRole'
				// A `tailCall`-shaped terminator or an `errorReturn` (a THROWING body's exit): both refuse,
				// and the terminator is exactly where the throwing one is caught.
				calling then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
				refusing then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
			end 'termRole'
		end 'terminated'
	end 'eachBlock'

	if hasPanic and hasStore 'panicRule'
		return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.panicWithStore, opCount: ops)
	end 'panicRule'
	if ops > MaxInlinedLeafOps 'tooBig'
		return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.tooManyOps, opCount: ops)
	end 'tooBig'

	return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.eligible, opCount: ops)
end 'scanLeafBody'

// What one body op costs against the budget: everything but a `param`, which becomes the call site's
// argument rather than an instruction. A panic op still counts — it is real code in the callee, and the
// budget is about how much of that this pass is willing to duplicate.
function countedBodyOps(role LeafOpRole) returns OpCount
	return 0 if role == LeafOpRole.parameter else 1
end 'countedBodyOps'

// The site census, accumulated across every caller — the pass's one report, and the number this rung is
// graded on. Site counts, not callee counts: the plan already carries the callee tallies, and a callee
// refused at 16 ops that nothing calls costs nothing.
type InlineLeafStats
	export var inlinedSites as SiteCount
	export var sitesRefusedByPanicRule as SiteCount
	export var sitesRefusedBySize as SiteCount
	export var sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget as SiteCount
	export var selfCallSites as SiteCount
	// EC7 — how many spliced parameter cascades this compile proved dead and did not copy. Reported for
	// `ReportedLargerLeafBudget`'s reason: it is the number the row is graded on, and it should come from the
	// compile being asked about rather than be re-derived from a corpus later.
	export var declinedParamGuards as SiteCount

	export static function create() returns InlineLeafStats
		return Self{inlinedSites: 0, sitesRefusedByPanicRule: 0, sitesRefusedBySize: 0, sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget: 0, selfCallSites: 0, declinedParamGuards: 0}
	end 'create'

	export function noteDeclinedParamGuard()
		self.declinedParamGuards = self.declinedParamGuards + 1
	end 'noteDeclinedParamGuard'

	export function noteInlined()
		self.inlinedSites = self.inlinedSites + 1
	end 'noteInlined'

	export function noteSelfCall()
		self.selfCallSites = self.selfCallSites + 1
	end 'noteSelfCall'

	export function notePanicRuleRefusal()
		self.sitesRefusedByPanicRule = self.sitesRefusedByPanicRule + 1
	end 'notePanicRuleRefusal'

	export function noteSizeRefusal(withinLargerBudget bool)
		self.sitesRefusedBySize = self.sitesRefusedBySize + 1
		if withinLargerBudget 'wouldFitLarger'
			self.sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget = self.sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget + 1
		end 'wouldFitLarger'
	end 'noteSizeRefusal'
end 'InlineLeafStats'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH OF THE CALLEE'S PARAMETER CASCADES *THIS* CALL SITE PROVES DEAD (EC7) — AND THE TWO SHAPES
// THE SPLICE NEEDS THAT ANSWER IN.**
//
// A narrowed parameter's entry cascade stands in front of EVERY caller, which is what makes it emittable at
// all (`InsertRangeChecks.guardRangedParams`). Inlined, it is no longer in front of every caller — it is in
// front of ONE, and that one may already know the argument is in range. Declining it here is the only place
// that fact can be spent: the callee cannot see it and the caller cannot reach into the callee.
//
// ⚠ **THE BLOCKS ARE `InsertRangeChecks`' ENUMERATION, NOT A WALK OF OUR OWN** — see
// `RangeCheckParamGuard.ownedBlockIds` for why re-deriving them from the CFG would mean modelling a
// terminator-less block's physical fall-through, which is a known-shaky corner of this tier.
type DeclinedGuards
	// Callee `BlockId` -> is this block part of a cascade this site declined? Such a block is copied by
	// NEITHER walk, exactly as a panic block is not — and this is asked FIRST, ahead of the panic test,
	// because a declined cascade's `__rc_panic` is one of its blocks.
	var declined as BoolArray
	// Callee `BlockId` -> where control goes instead when the cascade beginning at this block is declined;
	// every other id maps to itself. Written only for a declined cascade's HEAD.
	var forward as BlockIdArray
	// The callee's block-id space — held rather than spent at construction, because the columns are seeded
	// only if something is actually declined.
	var blockIdSpace as BlockId
	// The number of cascades, which BOUNDS the forwarding chase below: cascades chain (the k-th guard
	// splits the (k-1)-th's `__rc_ok`), so a declined guard's continuation can itself be a declined head.
	var guardCount as GuardCount
	// ⭐ Has anything been declined? It is ALSO the seeded bit for the two columns, and they are one fact:
	// the columns are written in exactly one place and it is the first decline.
	export var anyDeclined as bool

	// ⚠ `export` for ONE reader that is not a method: `sharedDeclinesNothing`, a module-level `let`, which
	// a file-private constructor may not initialize.
	export static function create(blockIdSpace BlockId, guardCount GuardCount) returns DeclinedGuards
		return Self{declined: sharedEmptyDeclineColumn, forward: sharedEmptyForwardColumn, blockIdSpace: blockIdSpace, guardCount: guardCount, anyDeclined: false}
	end 'create'

	function decline(guard RangeCheckParamGuard)
		if not self.anyDeclined 'stillShared'
			self.seedColumns()
			self.anyDeclined = true
		end 'stillShared'

		try self.forward.set(guard.headBlockId, value: guard.okBlockId) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.decline: cascade head {guard.headBlockId} is outside the callee's block-id space")
		for blockId in guard.ownedBlockIds 'eachOwnedBlock'
			try self.declined.set(blockId, value: true) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.decline: cascade block {blockId} is outside the callee's block-id space")
		end 'eachOwnedBlock'
	end 'decline'

	// COPY-ON-WRITE off the two shared empties, at the first decline and nowhere else. `forward` is seeded
	// to the IDENTITY so that "this block is not forwarded" needs no second column to say so.
	function seedColumns()
		var declineColumn = BoolArray.create()
		declineColumn.resize(self.blockIdSpace)
		self.declined = declineColumn
		var forwardColumn = BlockIdArray.create()
		forwardColumn.resize(self.blockIdSpace)
		for id in 0 upto self.blockIdSpace 'eachBlockId'
			try forwardColumn.set(id, value: id) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.seedColumns: forwardColumn.set OOB at {id} inside the space it was just resized to")
		end 'eachBlockId'
		self.forward = forwardColumn
	end 'seedColumns'

	export function isDeclined(calleeBlockId BlockId) returns bool
		if not self.anyDeclined 'noColumnToRead'
			return false
		end 'noColumnToRead'

		return try self.declined.get(calleeBlockId) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.isDeclined: callee block id {calleeBlockId} is outside the {self.declined.count()}-block id space this plan was built over")
	end 'isDeclined'

	// Where control ends up entering, following as many declined cascades as chain off `id`. The chase is
	// BOUNDED by the cascade count and panics rather than spinning: `forward` is written only at heads and
	// only ever forward along one chain, so a cycle would mean the record is not the chain it claims to be —
	// and the cost of believing it would be a splice branching into a block it never copied.
	export function entryFrom(id BlockId) returns BlockId
		if not self.anyDeclined 'noColumnToRead'
			return id
		end 'noColumnToRead'

		var target = id
		for _ in 0 upto self.guardCount + 1 'chase'
			let next = try self.forward.get(target) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.entryFrom: block id {target} is outside the {self.forward.count()}-block id space this plan was built over")
			if next == target 'settled'
				return target
			end 'settled'

			target = next
		end 'chase'

		panic("DeclinedGuards.entryFrom: the forwarding chain from block {id} is longer than the {self.guardCount} cascade(s) that can be on it — a cascade's `__rc_ok` has been recorded as its own head")
	end 'entryFrom'
end 'DeclinedGuards'

// ⭐ THE ONE PLAN EVERY SPLICE INTO A CALLEE WITH NO NARROWED PARAMETER ANSWERS FROM — which is the
// overwhelming majority of them. `sharedUnseededLeafMinter`'s pattern and its invariant: `decline` is the
// only thing that writes to a plan, and `declinedGuards` hands this one back exactly when the loop that
// would call it has nothing to iterate. Its `blockIdSpace`/`guardCount` are 0 and unread, because both
// columns and the chase are gated on `anyDeclined`, which is false here for ever.
let sharedDeclinesNothing = DeclinedGuards.create(0, guardCount: 0)

// ⭐ The columns ONE splice derives from its callee's block set, held together because every step of the
// splice reads at least two of them.
type SpliceBlocks
	// Callee `BlockId` → the caller `BlockId` a target resolves to: a COPIED block's fresh id, or — for a
	// panic block — the splice's single slow arm.
	export var blockSubst as BlockIdArray
	// Callee `BlockId` → is it a panic block? O(1) where re-deriving it would be a scan of the block's
	// ops per edge, and `IrModule.getBlockByIdIn` (the other route from an id to a block) is linear in
	// the function's block count.
	export var isPanic as BoolArray
	// The fresh blocks, in callee block order, skipping the panic blocks — so the copy walk can pair them
	// up positionally with the same skip rather than by a second id lookup.
	export var copiedRefs as BlockRefArray
	export var hasPanicBlock as bool
	// EC7 — the cascades this site declined. It rides here so the MINT walk and the COPY walk skip the same
	// blocks off the same column rather than each re-deciding it; that pairing is positional
	// (`copiedRefs`), and two answers to "is this block copied?" would silently pair a body with the wrong
	// fresh block.
	export var guards as DeclinedGuards

	static function create(blockIdSpace BlockId, guards DeclinedGuards) returns SpliceBlocks
		var blockSubst = BlockIdArray.create()
		blockSubst.resize(blockIdSpace)
		var isPanic = BoolArray.create()
		isPanic.resize(blockIdSpace)
		return Self{blockSubst: blockSubst, isPanic: isPanic, copiedRefs: BlockRefArray.create(), hasPanicBlock: false, guards: guards}
	end 'create'

	export function targetOf(calleeBlockId BlockId) returns BlockId
		return try self.blockSubst.get(calleeBlockId) otherwise panic("SpliceBlocks.targetOf: callee block id {calleeBlockId} is outside the {self.blockSubst.count()}-block id space this splice mapped")
	end 'targetOf'

	export function isPanicBlock(calleeBlockId BlockId) returns bool
		return try self.isPanic.get(calleeBlockId) otherwise panic("SpliceBlocks.isPanicBlock: callee block id {calleeBlockId} is outside the {self.isPanic.count()}-block id space this splice mapped")
	end 'isPanicBlock'
end 'SpliceBlocks'

// Expand every inlinable tiny-leaf call site in the module.
export function inlineLeaves(project Project, module StdModule)
	// ⚠ **THE NON-REFUSING NAME INDEX, for `PromoteStackRecords`' reason exactly.** A duplicate function
	// name is E4015's to report, and the site that owns that refusal runs LATER
	// (`DeadFunctionElimination` is where a runtime-symbol collision becomes visible). Raising it here
	// would move the refusal earlier and change which diagnostic a program gets, so this declines to
	// reason instead: with two bodies of one name, an inline could splice the wrong one.
	match FunctionNameIndex.build(module.functions) 'names'
		duplicateAt then return
		built(byName) then inlineWithNameIndex(project, module: module, byName: byName)
	end 'names'
end 'inlineLeaves'

function inlineWithNameIndex(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex)
	let plan = buildLeafPlan(module)
	if plan.eligibleCount == 0 'nothingToInline'
		logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: 0 site(s) inlined, 0 callee(s) eligible")
		return
	end 'nothingToInline'

	// Built ONCE, over the eligible callees alone — see `ValueOriginTable.indexRowsOfFunctions` for why
	// the alternative (`lookup` per copied value) is a quadratic against a table with one row per
	// value-producing op in the program.
	let origins = project.valueOrigins.indexRowsOfFunctions(plan.eligibleFuncs, funcCount: module.funcCount() as FuncIndex)
	var stats = InlineLeafStats.create()
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
		// ⭐ A STDLIB FUNCTION NO PATH FROM `main` REACHES IS SKIPPED — the same reachability fact
		// `insertRangeChecks` and `inlineManagedPrimitives` skip on. Here it is purely cost:
		// `eliminateDeadFunctions` drops the function before isel either way, and this pass registers no
		// `.rdata`, so skipping cannot move a golden.
		if project.stdlibFacts.unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'

		var inliner = LeafInliner.create(project, module: module, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, byName: byName, plan: plan, origins: origins, stats: stats)
		inliner.run()
	end 'eachFunc'

	let inlined = stats.inlinedSites
	let eligible = plan.eligibleCount
	let panicRefusals = stats.sitesRefusedByPanicRule
	let sizeRefusals = stats.sitesRefusedBySize
	let sizeRefusalsAtLargerBudget = stats.sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget
	let selfCalls = stats.selfCallSites
	let declinedGuards = stats.declinedParamGuards
	let calleesPanicRefused = plan.panicWithStoreCount
	let calleesSizeRefused = plan.tooManyOpsCount
	let calleesWithinLargerBudget = plan.tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: {inlined} site(s) inlined, {eligible} callee(s) eligible, {panicRefusals} refused-by-panic-rule ({calleesPanicRefused} callee(s)), {sizeRefusals} refused-by-size at {MaxInlinedLeafOps} ops ({calleesSizeRefused} callee(s)) of which {sizeRefusalsAtLargerBudget} site(s) / {calleesWithinLargerBudget} callee(s) a budget of {ReportedLargerLeafBudget} would admit, {selfCalls} self-call site(s), {declinedGuards} spliced parameter cascade(s) declined as provably dead")
end 'inlineWithNameIndex'

// Per-caller splicer: owns the function it rewrites plus the fresh-ValueId counter every copied body
// draws from, seeded past every id the function already uses.
type LeafInliner
	var project as Project
	var module as StdModule
	var func as IrFunction
	var funcIndex as FuncIndex
	var byName as FunctionNameIndex
	var plan as LeafPlan
	var origins as ValueOriginRowIndex
	var stats as InlineLeafStats
	// ⚠ SEEDED LAZILY, and that is a cost decision — `nextFreeValueId` walks every block arg and every
	// live op of the function and MATERIALIZES the live-op index array to do it, while this type is
	// constructed for every function in the module. `InlineManagedPrimitives.ids` carries the same shape
	// and the same measurement.
	var ids as ValueMinter
	var seeded as bool
	// The current block's sites, in ascending position. Reused across the function's blocks (truncated,
	// not reallocated) and shared-empty until the first site — see `sharedEmptySitePositions`.
	var sitePositions as BlockOpPosArray
	var siteCallees as FuncIndexArray
	var siteBuffersSeeded as bool

	static function create(project Project, module StdModule, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, byName FunctionNameIndex, plan LeafPlan, origins ValueOriginRowIndex, stats InlineLeafStats) returns LeafInliner
		return Self{
			project: project,
			module: module,
			func: func,
			funcIndex: funcIndex,
			byName: byName,
			plan: plan,
			origins: origins,
			stats: stats,
			ids: sharedUnseededLeafMinter,
			seeded: false,
			sitePositions: sharedEmptySitePositions,
			siteCallees: sharedEmptySiteCallees,
			siteBuffersSeeded: false
		}
	end 'create'

	// The function's fresh-ValueId counter, seeded past every id it already uses on first use.
	function valueIds() returns ValueMinter
		if self.seeded 'already'
			return self.ids
		end 'already'

		self.ids = ValueMinter.create(nextFreeValueId(self.module, func: self.func) as ParamCount)
		self.seeded = true
		return self.ids
	end 'valueIds'

	function seedSiteBuffers()
		if self.siteBuffersSeeded 'already'
			return
		end 'already'

		self.sitePositions = BlockOpPosArray.create()
		self.siteCallees = FuncIndexArray.create()
		self.siteBuffersSeeded = true
	end 'seedSiteBuffers'

	// ⭐ **THE BOUND IS SNAPSHOTTED, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THIS LOOP OFF ITS OWN OUTPUT — and, unlike
	// `InlineManagedPrimitives`, nothing is revisited at all.** A splice appends three kinds of block and
	// none of them can hold a site: a COPIED body is a leaf's body and holds no call by rule 1; the SLOW
	// arm holds the very call this splice moved, which re-inlining would loop on; and a CONTINUATION
	// holds ops of the ORIGINAL block, every site in which `inlineSitesIn` has already collected before
	// it split anything. So one pass over the original blocks IS the whole walk — no worklist, no
	// "already expanded" marker column, and no chain to follow.
	function run()
		let originalBlockCount = self.func.blockRefs.count()
		for i in 0 upto originalBlockCount 'eachBlock'
			let blockRef = try self.func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.run: blockRefs.get OOB at {i} despite the snapshot bound")
			self.inlineSitesIn(blockRef)
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'run'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE BLOCK, ONE FORWARD WALK, THEN A BACKWARD SPLIT PER SITE — never carve-then-rewalk.**
	// Splitting FORWARD hands each continuation the whole remaining tail and copies it again at the next
	// site: K sites in a B-op block cost Σ(B − pₖ) ≈ K·B/2, the product term `splitBlockInPlace`'s own
	// header names. Backwards, the k-th split moves exactly the ops between site k and site k+1, and the
	// total is B − p₀ — linear, whatever K is.
	//
	// ⭐ The block's EXIT lands on the LAST continuation for free rather than by a special case: the
	// first (rightmost) split takes the terminator, the conditional-branch slot and the outgoing edge
	// args, and every later split takes the `unset` that one left behind — which is exactly the
	// un-terminated state each intermediate continuation should be in when its own splice terminates it.
	function inlineSitesIn(blockRef BlockRef)
		let siteCount = self.collectSites(blockRef)
		if siteCount == 0 'noSite'
			return
		end 'noSite'

		// ⛔⛔ **THE MINTER IS SEEDED HERE, BEFORE THE FIRST SPLIT (EC1 review's ordering lesson).**
		// `nextFreeValueId` derives its floor from the function's LIVE ops, and every splice below drops
		// a call op out of `opRefs` — so between the drop and the moment the continuation takes that
		// call's result id as a block arg, the function's live set does not contain it. Seeded inside
		// that gap, the minter's floor can sit at or below the result id, and the slow arm's fresh
		// result comes back as the very id the continuation is about to define: two definitions of one
		// value, which this tier does not refuse and no golden reads back.
		_ = self.valueIds()

		var contRefs = BlockRefArray.create()
		contRefs.resize(siteCount)
		var k = siteCount
		while k > 0 'eachSiteBackwards'
			k = k - 1
			let at = try self.sitePositions.get(k) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.inlineSitesIn: sitePositions.get OOB at {k}")
			// Split immediately PAST the call, so the call stays the last op of the block on the left and
			// its continuation begins at the op that followed it.
			let cont = splitBlockInPlace(self.module, func: self.func, head: self.blockAt(blockRef), position: at + 1, label: InlineContLabel)
			try contRefs.set(k, value: registeredBlockRef(self.module, func: self.func, block: cont)) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.inlineSitesIn: contRefs.set OOB at {k}")
		end 'eachSiteBackwards'

		var pred = blockRef
		for i in 0 upto siteCount 'eachSite'
			let contRef = try contRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.inlineSitesIn: contRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			let calleeIndex = try self.siteCallees.get(i) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.inlineSitesIn: siteCallees.get OOB at {i}")
			self.spliceCallAtTailOf(pred, contRef: contRef, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
			pred = contRef
		end 'eachSite'
	end 'inlineSitesIn'

	// One forward walk of the block, recording every inlinable site and CENSUSING every other direct
	// call. It allocates nothing for a block with no site — which is nearly every block in the module —
	// because the buffers are shared-empty until `considerSite` seeds them.
	//
	// ⚠ The `StdOp` match is EXHAUSTIVE: `call` is named and everything else is covered by range arms,
	// so a variant appended past the end of the union fails to compile here until it is consciously
	// placed. A terminator is never a `call`, so the half this walk omits holds no site.
	function collectSites(blockRef BlockRef) returns SiteCount
		if self.siteBuffersSeeded 'reuse'
			self.sitePositions.truncate(0)
			self.siteCallees.truncate(0)
		end 'reuse'

		let opCount = self.blockAt(blockRef).opRefs.count()
		for pos in 0 upto opCount 'eachOp'
			match self.opAt(blockRef, pos: pos) 'kind'
				const to param then break 'kind'
				call(_, callee, _) then self.considerSite(pos, callee: callee)
				retVoid to osEnterBackgroundPriority then break 'kind'
				osFileOpenWriteExecutable then break 'kind'
				osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then break 'kind'
				loadAcquire or storeRelease then break 'kind'
				osHandleFileType then break 'kind'
			end 'kind'
		end 'eachOp'
		return self.sitePositions.count()
	end 'collectSites'

	// ⚠ **THE CALLEE IS A RAW `ByteArray` NAME AND IS COMPARED AS BYTES, NEVER PARSED.** Names like
	// `__paramDefault#emitCmp#6` and `<Type>.<method>` reach this tier verbatim, and one of the corpus's
	// own generated names carries a space.
	//
	// A callee the index does not know is a RUNTIME body — `__mm_*`, `__str_*`, `__managed_*`, the
	// synthesized destructor cascades — which are installed AFTER the pipeline and so are absent from
	// `module.functions` at this point. They are therefore never inlined BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by
	// a roster this file would have to keep. (`mrt_panic` is not a call at all: it is an `osPanic` op.)
	function considerSite(pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray)
		let calleeIndex = try self.byName.byName.get(callee) otherwise return
		if calleeIndex == self.funcIndex 'selfCall'
			// A self-call cannot be spliced (the body would contain its own copy), and the leaf rule has
			// already refused the function for holding a call at all — so this is counted rather than
			// tested for: the two rules agree, and the census says how often.
			self.stats.noteSelfCall()
			return
		end 'selfCall'

		match self.plan.verdictOf(calleeIndex) 'verdict'
			eligible then self.recordSite(pos, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
			panicWithStore then self.stats.notePanicRuleRefusal()
			tooManyOps then self.stats.noteSizeRefusal(self.plan.isWithinLargerBudget(calleeIndex))
			notALeaf then break 'verdict'
		end 'verdict'
	end 'considerSite'

	function recordSite(pos BlockOpPos, calleeIndex FuncIndex)
		self.seedSiteBuffers()
		self.sitePositions.push(pos)
		self.siteCallees.push(calleeIndex)
	end 'recordSite'

	// The call is the LAST op of `predRef` by construction — `inlineSitesIn` split immediately past it.
	// It is read, spliced, and then dropped; only the slow arm re-issues it, verbatim, with a fresh
	// result.
	//
	// ⚠ The dropped op is left behind as an ORPHAN in `module.ops`. That is the tier's own model
	// (`IrModule.ops` is append-only and retains what block-rebuilding passes replace), and it is why
	// every reader asks `liveOpIndices` rather than scanning the array.
	function spliceCallAtTailOf(predRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, calleeIndex FuncIndex)
		let tail = self.blockAt(predRef).opRefs.count() - 1
		match self.opAt(predRef, pos: tail) 'site'
			const to param then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			call(resultId, callee, args) then self.splice(predRef, contRef: contRef, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId)
			retVoid to osEnterBackgroundPriority then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			loadAcquire or storeRelease then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			osHandleFileType then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
		end 'site'
		var pred = self.blockAt(predRef)
		pred.opRefs.truncate(tail)
		self.stats.noteInlined()
	end 'spliceCallAtTailOf'

	// The backstop the two non-`call` arms above share — ONE site, because they say one thing: the split
	// cut immediately PAST the call, so the call is the last op of the block on the left, and anything
	// else there means a coordinate moved between the split and this read.
	//
	// ⚠ It is a FUNCTION THAT PANICS rather than a function returning the message, because `panic` takes
	// a string LITERAL (E3005 refuses an expression), so a shared message cannot be shared as a value.
	function refuseNotTheSplitCall()
		panic("LeafInliner.spliceCallAtTailOf: the last op of a spliced block of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is not the call the split cut past")
	end 'refuseNotTheSplitCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SPLICE.** `predRef` ends in the call (about to be dropped); `contRef` holds everything
	// that followed it, and — if this is the block's last site — the block's whole exit.
	function splice(predRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, calleeIndex FuncIndex, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId)
		let calleeFunc = self.module.getFunc(calleeIndex)
		if args.count() != calleeFunc.paramCount 'arity'
			panic("LeafInliner.splice: '{String.from(self.func.name)}' calls '{String.from(callee)}' with {args.count()} argument(s) against {calleeFunc.paramCount} parameter(s) — `lowerCall` emits arguments in declaration order and fills every slot, the trailing layout/witness parameters of a generic callee included, so a mismatch here is a malformed call rather than a shape to decline")
		end 'arity'

		let subst = self.buildValueColumn(calleeFunc, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, args: args)
		let blocks = self.mintCopiedBlocks(calleeFunc, guards: self.declinedGuards(calleeFunc, args: args))
		// ⭐ THE SLOW ARM — one per splice, minted only when the callee has a panic block to redirect.
		// A panic block always HAS a predecessor (it is a guard's `condBranch` target, or a user
		// `panic(…)`'s own block), so this cannot mint an arm nothing branches to; and were it ever to,
		// the cost is a dead block holding the call, not a wrong answer.
		if blocks.hasPanicBlock 'redirectPanics'
			self.mintSlowArm(contRef, callee: callee, args: args, calleeFunc: calleeFunc, blocks: blocks)
		end 'redirectPanics'

		self.attachContinuationArg(contRef, calleeFunc: calleeFunc, resultId: resultId)
		self.copyBodies(calleeFunc, contRef: contRef, blocks: blocks, subst: subst)
		self.project.valueOrigins.copyRows(self.origins, fromFunc: calleeIndex, toFunc: self.funcIndex, subst: subst, calleeParamCount: calleeFunc.paramCount)

		// The head's own exit was moved onto the continuation by the split, so it is un-terminated here
		// and carries no edge. The copied entry block takes no block args (`scanLeafBody` refuses a
		// callee whose entry carries phis), so this branch carries none either.
		self.module.setTerminator(self.blockAt(predRef), op: StdOp.branch(blocks.targetOf(self.calleeEntryBlockId(calleeFunc))))
	end 'splice'

	// ⭐ **THE DENSE VALUE COLUMN, TOTAL OVER THE CALLEE'S WHOLE ID SPACE — parameters to the site's
	// arguments, everything else to a fresh caller id.**
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT MINTS FOR EVERY NON-PARAMETER INDEX, NOT ONLY FOR THE VALUES SOME OP DEFINES, AND THAT IS
	// WHAT MAKES IT TOTAL RATHER THAN NEARLY TOTAL.** v1's column carried a `REMAP_INVALID` sentinel for
	// the ids it did not bind and rested on nothing ever reading one; here there is no unbound id to
	// read, so `remapStdOpForClone` can look every value up STRICTLY and a column built against the wrong
	// function is a panic instead of a silently wrong operand. The price is the handful of ids that go to
	// values in a DROPPED panic block, or to a gap an earlier pass's orphaned op left in the numbering —
	// a couple per splice, against a caller value space the register allocator sizes its columns by.
	//
	// ⚠ Parameters occupy `0..paramCount-1` BY CONVENTION (`IrValueId.maxon`, `emitParams`), which is why
	// this is a range rather than a scan for `param` ops. `copyBlockOps` CHECKS each `param` op against
	// it as it skips the op, so the convention is verified where it is spent rather than assumed here.
	function buildValueColumn(calleeFunc IrFunction, calleeIndex FuncIndex, args ValueIdArray) returns ValueIdArray
		let bound = self.plan.valueBoundOf(calleeIndex)
		var subst = ValueIdArray.create()
		subst.resize(bound)
		for i in 0 upto calleeFunc.paramCount 'eachParam'
			let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.buildValueColumn: args.get OOB at {i} despite the arity check")
			try subst.set(i, value: arg) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.buildValueColumn: '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' declares {calleeFunc.paramCount} parameter(s) but its value space is only {bound} wide — parameters occupy ids 0..paramCount-1 by the Std ABI's convention")
		end 'eachParam'
		for v in valueIdAtParamPosition(calleeFunc.paramCount) upto bound 'eachBodyValue'
			try subst.set(v, value: self.valueIds().mint()) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.buildValueColumn: subst.set OOB at {v}")
		end 'eachBodyValue'
		return subst
	end 'buildValueColumn'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CASCADES THIS SITE DECLINES.** Empty for every callee with no narrowed parameter, which is
	// almost all of them — and that empty `paramGuards` list is what keeps the cost off the common splice:
	// the loop below runs zero times and no proof is asked.
	function declinedGuards(calleeFunc IrFunction, args ValueIdArray) returns DeclinedGuards
		if calleeFunc.paramGuards.isEmpty() 'noNarrowedParameter'
			return sharedDeclinesNothing
		end 'noNarrowedParameter'

		var declined = DeclinedGuards.create(calleeFunc.nextBlockId, guardCount: calleeFunc.paramGuards.count())
		for guard in calleeFunc.paramGuards 'eachGuard'
			if not self.callerProvesArg(args, guard: guard) 'notProvenHere'
				continue
			end 'notProvenHere'

			declined.decline(guard)
			self.stats.noteDeclinedParamGuard()
		end 'eachGuard'
		return declined
	end 'declinedGuards'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES *THIS* FRAME ALREADY KNOW THE ARGUMENT IS IN THE CALLEE'S RANGE? — THE TWO PROOFS, AND
	// THERE ARE EXACTLY TWO.** Both are compared with the containment every range door in the compiler
	// already uses (`TypeRules`), so this pass reaches no verdict of its own and cannot come to disagree
	// with the guard it is declining.
	function callerProvesArg(args ValueIdArray, guard RangeCheckParamGuard) returns bool
		let arg = try args.get(guard.index) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.callerProvesArg: a callee guarding parameter {guard.index} is called from '{String.from(self.func.name)}' with {args.count()} argument(s) — the arity check has already refused a mismatch")
		return self.callerParamProves(arg, alias: guard.alias) or self.countedLoopProves(arg, alias: guard.alias)
	end 'callerProvesArg'

	// **PROOF ONE: THE ARGUMENT IS ONE OF THIS FUNCTION'S OWN NARROWED PARAMETERS.** Its cascade stands at
	// this function's entry, which dominates every call the body makes, so the value in hand has already
	// passed a range — and if that range lies inside the callee's, the callee's cascade cannot fail either.
	// This is the shape EC6's survey measured at 3 of `regMaskContains`' 14 sites (`hintIsTakeable`
	// forwarding a `RegNum` parameter to two more calls, and `assertOneCallClobber`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A REASSIGNED PARAMETER IS EXCLUDED, AND IT IS NOT A CORNER CASE.** `reassignedParamMask` names
	// the parameters that arrive as a CELL ADDRESS (PBR-2): the range describes what the cell HOLDS, the
	// body may store a new value into it, and the entry guard reads THROUGH the cell rather than checking
	// the pointer (`GuardedOperand.pointeeOf`). The id would still match here, and matching it would spend
	// a proof about the cell's arriving contents on whatever the body last wrote into it.
	//
	// ⚠ A LINEAR SCAN over this function's own cascades, which is one per narrowed parameter and never
	// more than its arity — the bound `entryGuardAlreadyCovers` and `paramAliasOf` already carry.
	function callerParamProves(arg ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		if arg >= valueIdAtParamPosition(self.func.paramCount) 'notAParameterOfThisFrame'
			return false
		end 'notAParameterOfThisFrame'
		let position = paramPositionOfValueId(arg)
		if maskHasParam(self.func.reassignedParamMask, param: position) 'arrivesAsACellAddress'
			return false
		end 'arrivesAsACellAddress'

		for ownGuard in self.func.paramGuards 'eachOwnGuard'
			if ownGuard.index == position 'thisParameter'
				return rangeCoversRange(alias, source: ownGuard.alias)
			end 'thisParameter'
		end 'eachOwnGuard'

		return false
	end 'callerParamProves'

	// **PROOF TWO: A COUNTED LOOP PROVED AN INTERVAL FOR IT (EC7 layer 1).** `for r in 0 upto
	// RegisterFileSize` gives its counter a range nothing NAMES, which the parser records against this
	// function's own `ValueId` numbering and `LowerMaxonToStd` carries across verbatim (that pass does not
	// renumber). It is the proof the four hottest `regMaskContains` sites need and the one no declared type
	// can supply — the counter's written type is the bare `int` its bounds were written at.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PROBE IS MADE HERE, BEHIND A CALLEE THAT ACTUALLY GUARDS A PARAMETER**, and not hoisted into
	// the per-caller constructor: a `LeafInliner` is built for every function in the module and nearly none
	// of them splices anything that guards anything, so a probe up there would be one map lookup per
	// function for an answer almost nobody spends.
	function countedLoopProves(arg ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		let checks = try self.project.rangeChecks.get(self.func.name) otherwise return false
		let proven = try checks.provenValueRanges.get(arg) otherwise return false
		return rangeCoversIntBounds(alias, low: proven.low, high: proven.high)
	end 'countedLoopProves'

	function calleeEntryBlockId(calleeFunc IrFunction) returns BlockId
		let entryRef = try calleeFunc.blockRefs.get(0) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.calleeEntryBlockId: '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' has no entry block, which `scanLeafBody` refuses")
		let entry = try self.module.blocks.get(entryRef) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.calleeEntryBlockId: entry blockRef {entryRef} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
		return entry.id
	end 'calleeEntryBlockId'

	// Mint one fresh caller block per NON-PANIC callee block, filling the block column and recording each
	// new block's ref in callee order.
	//
	// A panic block's column entry stays 0 until `mintSlowArm` fills it; nothing reads it in between,
	// because the ops that name it are copied afterwards.
	function mintCopiedBlocks(calleeFunc IrFunction, guards DeclinedGuards) returns SpliceBlocks
		var blocks = SpliceBlocks.create(calleeFunc.nextBlockId, guards: guards)
		let entryId = guards.entryFrom(self.calleeEntryBlockId(calleeFunc))
		for i in 0 upto calleeFunc.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
			let calleeBlockRef = try calleeFunc.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.mintCopiedBlocks: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			let calleeBlock = try self.module.blocks.get(calleeBlockRef) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.mintCopiedBlocks: blockRef {calleeBlockRef} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
			// ⭐ **A DECLINED CASCADE'S BLOCKS ARE NOT COPIED, AND THIS IS ASKED AHEAD OF THE PANIC TEST**
			// (EC7): its `__rc_panic` is one of them, and a slow arm minted to catch a panic nothing can
			// reach would be a dead block holding a REAL CALL — enough to keep the callee alive through
			// dead-function elimination and to give this frame outgoing-argument space for a call that
			// never runs.
			if guards.isDeclined(calleeBlock.id) 'insideADeclinedCascade'
				continue
			end 'insideADeclinedCascade'
			if self.blockHoldsAPanic(calleeBlock, calleeFunc: calleeFunc) 'panicBlock'
				blocks.hasPanicBlock = true
				try blocks.isPanic.set(calleeBlock.id, value: true) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.mintCopiedBlocks: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' has id {calleeBlock.id}, outside '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}'s id space of {calleeFunc.nextBlockId}")
				continue
			end 'panicBlock'

			// The label marks the block the splice ENTERS, which is the callee's entry only while no cascade
			// in front of it was declined — `__il_body` is a claim about this copy, not about the callee, so
			// it follows the entry rather than position 0.
			let fresh = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineBodyLabel if calleeBlock.id == entryId else calleeBlock.label)
			try blocks.blockSubst.set(calleeBlock.id, value: fresh.id) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.mintCopiedBlocks: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' has id {calleeBlock.id}, outside '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}'s id space of {calleeFunc.nextBlockId}")
			let freshRef = registeredBlockRef(self.module, func: self.func, block: fresh)
			blocks.copiedRefs.push(freshRef)
			// Coverage follows the code: a copied block's source footprint is the CALLEE block's, which is
			// where its ops were written. `addBlock` pushed an empty entry, parallel to `blocks`.
			self.carryBlockSourceInfo(calleeBlockRef, to: freshRef)
		end 'eachBlock'

		self.mapDeclinedCascadeHeads(calleeFunc, blocks: blocks, guards: guards)
		return blocks
	end 'mintCopiedBlocks'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY EDGE INTO A DECLINED CASCADE'S HEAD LANDS ON THE BLOCK THAT CASCADE WOULD HAVE PASSED
	// CONTROL TO, and it is spelled as a `blockSubst` entry rather than as a rewrite of the edges.** The
	// substitution column is what `remapStdOpForClone` reads for every terminator it clones AND what
	// `targetOf` answers the splice's own entry branch from — so writing the redirect there redirects both,
	// and there is no list of edge kinds to keep in step.
	//
	// The cascade's OTHER blocks keep the `0` a resized column starts with, and nothing reads it: a
	// `__rc_chk` is named only by its cascade's head or by the `__rc_chk` before it, and the `__rc_panic`
	// only by those — every one of them declined, and none of them copied.
	function mapDeclinedCascadeHeads(calleeFunc IrFunction, blocks SpliceBlocks, guards DeclinedGuards)
		if not guards.anyDeclined 'nothingDeclined'
			return
		end 'nothingDeclined'

		for guard in calleeFunc.paramGuards 'eachGuard'
			if not guards.isDeclined(guard.headBlockId) 'stillEmitted'
				continue
			end 'stillEmitted'

			let landing = guards.entryFrom(guard.okBlockId)
			try blocks.blockSubst.set(guard.headBlockId, value: blocks.targetOf(landing)) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.mapDeclinedCascadeHeads: cascade head {guard.headBlockId} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is outside its own block-id space")
		end 'eachGuard'
	end 'mapDeclinedCascadeHeads'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE PANIC REDIRECT.** One block per splice that re-issues the ORIGINAL call — same callee,
	// same argument values, a fresh result — and branches to the continuation carrying it. Every panic
	// block of the callee points here.
	//
	// The callee is PURE (the eligibility rule), so re-running it from the start on the same arguments
	// takes the same path and reaches the same panic, with the same interned message and — because it is
	// a real call — the same FRAME. That is why `range-check-panic`'s `in clamp / in main / in mrt_start`
	// is byte-identical with this pass on and off, and why the ~86 `Stack trace:` cases across 17 spec
	// files are this rule's gate rather than its risk.
	//
	// ⚠ The argument array is SHARED with the dropped call op rather than copied — the op is an orphan
	// and nothing rewrites either. `InlineManagedPrimitives.openPlain` shares the same way.
	function mintSlowArm(contRef BlockRef, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, calleeFunc IrFunction, blocks SpliceBlocks)
		let contId = self.blockAt(contRef).id
		var slow = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineSlowLabel)
		let slowResult = self.valueIds().mint()
		self.module.appendOp(slow, op: StdOp.call(slowResult, callee: callee, args: args))
		self.module.setTerminator(slow, op: StdOp.branch(contId))
		attachEdge(slow, targetId: contId, argIds: self.returnedValues(calleeFunc, valueId: slowResult))

		for i in 0 upto blocks.isPanic.count() 'eachCalleeBlockId'
			if not blocks.isPanicBlock(i) 'notAPanicBlock'
				continue
			end 'notAPanicBlock'

			try blocks.blockSubst.set(i, value: slow.id) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.mintSlowArm: blockSubst.set OOB at {i}")
		end 'eachCalleeBlockId'
	end 'mintSlowArm'

	// The value list a return into the continuation carries: the one value for a value-returning callee,
	// and NOTHING for a void one. ONE function feeding all THREE users of that arity — the slow arm's
	// edge, every copied `ret`/`retVoid`'s edge, and the continuation's own block-arg list — so the
	// positional `blockArgs[k] ↔ edge.argIds[k]` pairing cannot be built from two readings of the same
	// return type.
	function returnedValues(calleeFunc IrFunction, valueId ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
		var out = ValueIdArray.create()
		match calleeFunc.returnType 'rt'
			void then break 'rt'
			value then out.push(valueId)
		end 'rt'
		return out
	end 'returnedValues'

	// ⭐ **THE CONTINUATION'S SINGLE BLOCK ARG IS THE CALL'S ORIGINAL RESULT ID**, which is the whole
	// reason no op after the site has to change: every one of them still names the value it always did.
	// Its TYPE is the callee's declared return type — what decides the value's register FILE on x64 and
	// arm64 and its valtype on wasm, so a float-returning leaf's result stays in an XMM.
	//
	// A VOID callee gets none. Its `resultId` is a placeholder the lowering mints and nothing reads (a
	// void call's result is not a value any op can name), and there is no `ret v` in the body to feed a
	// block arg with.
	//
	// A void callee touches nothing here — no array is built for it, which is the difference between
	// "the continuation declares no args" and "it declares an empty list", and this pass makes thousands
	// of continuations.
	function attachContinuationArg(contRef BlockRef, calleeFunc IrFunction, resultId ValueId)
		match calleeFunc.returnType 'rt'
			void then break 'rt'
			value(inner) then attachBlockArgs(self.blockAt(contRef), valueIds: self.returnedValues(calleeFunc, valueId: resultId), argType: inner)
		end 'rt'
	end 'attachContinuationArg'

	// Copy every non-panic block's phis, ops and exit into the fresh blocks `mintCopiedBlocks` minted, in
	// the same order and skipping the same blocks — so the two walks pair up positionally.
	function copyBodies(calleeFunc IrFunction, contRef BlockRef, blocks SpliceBlocks, subst ValueIdArray)
		var copied = 0 as BlockRefIndex
		for i in 0 upto calleeFunc.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
			let calleeBlockRef = try calleeFunc.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.copyBodies: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			let calleeBlock = try self.module.blocks.get(calleeBlockRef) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.copyBodies: blockRef {calleeBlockRef} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
			if blocks.guards.isDeclined(calleeBlock.id) 'insideADeclinedCascade'
				continue
			end 'insideADeclinedCascade'
			if blocks.isPanicBlock(calleeBlock.id) 'panicBlock'
				continue
			end 'panicBlock'

			let freshRef = try blocks.copiedRefs.get(copied) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.copyBodies: copiedRefs.get OOB at {copied} — `mintCopiedBlocks` and this walk skip the same blocks in the same order")
			copied = copied + 1
			self.copyBlockArgs(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, subst: subst)
			self.copyBlockOps(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, subst: subst, blocks: blocks, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
			self.copyBlockExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, contRef: contRef, subst: subst, blocks: blocks, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'copyBodies'

	// A copied block's phis, through the value column. Their TYPES come across verbatim — a phi's type is
	// a property of the value it carries, not of the function it is in.
	function copyBlockArgs(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, subst ValueIdArray)
		if calleeBlock.blockArgs.count() == 0 'noPhis'
			return
		end 'noPhis'

		var blockArgs = IrBlockArgArray.create()
		for arg in calleeBlock.blockArgs 'each'
			blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(clonedValue(arg.valueId, subst: subst), argType: arg.argType))
		end 'each'
		var fresh = self.blockAt(freshRef)
		fresh.blockArgs = blockArgs
	end 'copyBlockArgs'

	function copyBlockOps(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, subst ValueIdArray, blocks SpliceBlocks, calleeFunc IrFunction)
		for opIdx in calleeBlock.opRefs 'eachOp'
			// ⚠ **THE OP IS READ THROUGH `opAtIndex` AND THE CLONE IS BOUND BEFORE THE APPEND, AND BOTH ARE
			// E3070's DOING.** `self.module.ops.get(...)` inline here BORROWS `module`, and `appendOp`
			// mutates it — the self-compile refuses that (`cannot mutate 'module' via 'appendOp' while it
			// is borrowed`) where the bootstrap does not, so this shape is what the SELF-COMPILE gates.
			let op = self.opAtIndex(opIdx, ofFunc: calleeFunc)
			if leafOpRole(op) == LeafOpRole.parameter 'parameterOp'
				assertParamOpMatchesConvention(op, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
				continue
			end 'parameterOp'

			let cloned = remapStdOpForClone(op, valueSubst: subst, blockSubst: blocks.blockSubst)
			self.module.appendOp(self.blockAt(freshRef), op: cloned)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'copyBlockOps'

	// ⭐ **A `ret` BECOMES A BRANCH TO THE CONTINUATION carrying what it was returning; every other
	// terminator is cloned with its block targets remapped.** A callee with two returns therefore gives
	// the continuation two incoming edges into one block arg, which is precisely what a phi is for.
	function copyBlockExit(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, subst ValueIdArray, blocks SpliceBlocks, calleeFunc IrFunction)
		if not calleeBlock.hasTerminatorOp() 'oplessTerminator'
			self.carryOplessExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
			return
		end 'oplessTerminator'

		let term = self.opAtIndex(calleeBlock.terminatorIndex(), ofFunc: calleeFunc)
		match term 'exit'
			const to mod then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			condBranch to branch then self.cloneExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, term: term, subst: subst, blocks: blocks)
			ret(retValId) then self.returnToContinuation(freshRef, contRef: contRef, argIds: self.returnedValues(calleeFunc, valueId: clonedValue(retValId, subst: subst)))
			param to call then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			retVoid then self.returnToContinuation(freshRef, contRef: contRef, argIds: ValueIdArray.create())
			globalAddr to osFreePages then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			switch then self.cloneExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, term: term, subst: subst, blocks: blocks)
			witnessCall to osEnterBackgroundPriority then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			loadAcquire or storeRelease then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			osHandleFileType then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
		end 'exit'
	end 'copyBlockExit'

	// ⚠ **A BLOCK CAN HAVE NO TERMINATOR OP AT ALL, AND THAT IS NOT A MALFORMED BLOCK.** The parser marks
	// the continuation after a fully-terminated `if`/`else` — or after a `match` whose every arm breaks
	// or panics — `Terminator.dead`: unreachable, and therefore terminated by DECLARATION rather than by
	// an op (`IrBlock.Terminator`). Copying such a block means copying the SLOT, which is exactly what
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.carryOplessTerminator` does at the tier boundary and for the same reason —
	// `IrModule.assertAllBlocksTerminated` demands every block commit to one of the four states.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE BLOCK IS COPIED RATHER THAN DROPPED, unlike a panic block**, because a panic block is one
	// this pass has a REPLACEMENT for (the slow arm) while a dead block has none: if a copied block's
	// terminator names it, dropping it would leave that branch pointing at a block id the column never
	// mapped. It costs the caller a few unreachable ops, which the block budget already bounds.
	// MEASURED: `match-statements.break-only-arms-with-panic-arm` crashed the compiler here before this
	// arm existed — `Holder.assertUsable`'s `afterbreak` is exactly this shape.
	function carryOplessExit(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, calleeFunc IrFunction)
		var fresh = self.blockAt(freshRef)
		match calleeBlock.terminator 'carry'
			unset then panic("LeafInliner.carryOplessExit: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is still `Terminator.unset` — `assertAllBlocksTerminated` refuses that at the end of the parse")
			dead then fresh.terminator = Terminator.dead
			fallthrough then panic("LeafInliner.carryOplessExit: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is marked `fallthrough`, which is a TARGET-tier layout decision — no Std block carries one")
			opIndex then panic("LeafInliner.carryOplessExit: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' names a terminator op, which `hasTerminatorOp` has already excluded")
		end 'carry'
	end 'carryOplessExit'

	// A terminator that stays inside the copied body: `branch`, `condBranch` and `switch`.
	//
	// ⚠ The callee block's OWN outgoing edges are rebuilt as a FRESH array — never mutated in place, per
	// `IrBlock`'s phi-model rule: the three tiers share one copy of that model and it reaches back into
	// the parse memo, so an in-place write corrupts every later compile of the project with nothing
	// downstream that re-reads it to notice. An edge into a PANIC block is DROPPED rather than
	// redirected: the slow arm declares no block args, and `branchEdges` is not a successor list, so an
	// edge exists only where phis are carried.
	function cloneExit(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, term StdOp, subst ValueIdArray, blocks SpliceBlocks)
		let cloned = remapStdOpForClone(term, valueSubst: subst, blockSubst: blocks.blockSubst)
		self.module.setTerminator(self.blockAt(freshRef), op: cloned)

		var edges = BranchEdgeArray.create()
		for edge in calleeBlock.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
			if blocks.isPanicBlock(edge.targetBlockId) 'toThePanicArm'
				continue
			end 'toThePanicArm'

			var argIds = ValueIdArray.create()
			for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
				argIds.push(clonedValue(argId, subst: subst))
			end 'eachArg'
			edges.push(BranchEdge.create(blocks.targetOf(edge.targetBlockId), argIds: argIds))
		end 'eachEdge'
		var fresh = self.blockAt(freshRef)
		fresh.branchEdges = edges
	end 'cloneExit'

	function returnToContinuation(freshRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, argIds ValueIdArray)
		let contId = self.blockAt(contRef).id
		self.module.setTerminator(self.blockAt(freshRef), op: StdOp.branch(contId))
		attachEdge(self.blockAt(freshRef), targetId: contId, argIds: argIds)
	end 'returnToContinuation'

	// Does this block hold a panic op? A panic is a BODY op (followed by an unreachable return — see
	// `InsertRangeChecks.emitPanicBlock`), so the terminator half is not consulted; `scanLeafBody` panics
	// if one ever reaches a terminator slot.
	function blockHoldsAPanic(block IrBlock, calleeFunc IrFunction) returns bool
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			let op = self.opAtIndex(opIdx, ofFunc: calleeFunc)
			if leafOpRole(op) == LeafOpRole.panicking 'panics'
				return true
			end 'panics'
		end 'eachOp'
		return false
	end 'blockHoldsAPanic'

	// --- block/op access ---

	function blockAt(blockRef BlockRef) returns IrBlock
		return try self.module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("LeafInliner: blockRef {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
	end 'blockAt'

	function opAt(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns StdOp
		let opIdx = try self.blockAt(blockRef).opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("LeafInliner: block {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' has no op at position {pos}")
		return self.opAtIndex(opIdx, ofFunc: self.func)
	end 'opAt'

	// ⚠ **EVERY OP READ ON A MUTATING PATH GOES THROUGH HERE, and that is E3070 rather than tidiness.**
	// A `self.module.ops.get(...)` written inline borrows `module` for as long as the value lives, and
	// this pass appends ops and blocks to `module` in the very next breath — which the SELF-COMPILE
	// refuses and the bootstrap does not. Reading through a function ends the borrow at the return, the
	// same discipline `PrimitiveInliner.opAt` follows.
	function opAtIndex(opIdx OpIndex, ofFunc IrFunction) returns StdOp
		return try self.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("LeafInliner: op index {opIdx} named by a block of '{String.from(ofFunc.name)}' is not in module.ops")
	end 'opAtIndex'

	// A copied block's source footprint. Read and written in ONE call for `opAtIndex`'s reason: the read
	// borrows `module` and the write mutates it.
	function carryBlockSourceInfo(sourceRef BlockRef, to BlockRef)
		let sourceInfo = try self.module.blockSourceInfo.get(sourceRef) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.carryBlockSourceInfo: blockSourceInfo has no entry for blockRef {sourceRef}")
		try self.module.blockSourceInfo.set(to, value: sourceInfo) otherwise panic("LeafInliner.carryBlockSourceInfo: blockSourceInfo.set OOB at {to}")
	end 'carryBlockSourceInfo'
end 'LeafInliner'

// ⭐ **THE STD-ABI CONVENTION, CHECKED WHERE IT IS SPENT.** `buildValueColumn` maps ids
// `0..paramCount-1` straight onto the call's arguments instead of scanning for `param` ops, which is
// right exactly while a `param` op's result id IS its index (`LowerMaxonToStd.emitParams` emits
// `StdOp.param(i as ValueId, index: i, …)`, and `InsertRangeChecks.rangedParamValue` rests on the same
// identity). A body that broke it would splice silently, reading the caller's same-numbered values —
// v1's `bindParamRemap` header records exactly that failure, one cause further out.
function assertParamOpMatchesConvention(op StdOp, calleeFunc IrFunction)
	match op 'p'
		const to ret then refuseNotAParamOp()
		param(resultId, index, _) then requireParamValueIsItsIndex(resultId, index: index, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
		call to osEnterBackgroundPriority then refuseNotAParamOp()
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then refuseNotAParamOp()
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then refuseNotAParamOp()
		loadAcquire or storeRelease then refuseNotAParamOp()
		osHandleFileType then refuseNotAParamOp()
	end 'p'
end 'assertParamOpMatchesConvention'

// The backstop every non-terminator arm of `copyBlockExit` shares — ONE string, for `notTheSplitCall`'s
// reason. Which op it was is not worth naming: any of them in a terminator slot is the same broken
// block, and `scanLeafBody` has already refused every op a leaf may not hold.
function refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc IrFunction)
	panic("LeafInliner.copyBlockExit: an op that is not a terminator is in the terminator slot of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}'")
end 'refuseNotATerminator'

// The backstop the two non-`param` arms share — ONE string, for `notTheSplitCall`'s reason.
function refuseNotAParamOp()
	panic("assertParamOpMatchesConvention: asked of an op that is not a `param` — its one caller has already classified it as one through `leafOpRole`")
end 'refuseNotAParamOp'

function requireParamValueIsItsIndex(resultId ValueId, index ParamCount, calleeFunc IrFunction)
	if resultId != valueIdAtParamPosition(index) 'conventionBroken'
		panic("LeafInliner: parameter {index} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' defines value {resultId} — the Std ABI reserves ids 0..paramCount-1 for the parameters IN ORDER, and the splice's value column maps them onto the call's arguments by that identity")
	end 'conventionBroken'
	if index >= calleeFunc.paramCount 'pastTheDeclaration'
		panic("LeafInliner: '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' has a `param` op for position {index} but declares {calleeFunc.paramCount} parameter(s)")
	end 'pastTheDeclaration'
end 'requireParamValueIsItsIndex'
